<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128161;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> The book we read in this unit is:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Critique of Pure Reason</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Groundwork for the Metaphysic of Morals</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">Critique of Practical Reason</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">The Metaphysics of Morals</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Our reading is the Groundwork for the Metaphysic of Morals (1785), Kant's short introduction to the foundations of his moral philosophy.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">These are all real works by Kant, but this unit reads only one: the short book that lays the groundwork for his moral philosophy.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> Kant argues that a genuine moral philosophy must be derived from:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">Careful observation of how human beings actually behave</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">Pure reason, independent of empirical facts about human nature</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">The emotional responses that guide everyday moral judgments</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">Religious revelation and tradition</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Kant argues that moral philosophy must be grounded a priori, in pure reason. Moral law has to bind every rational being, so it cannot rest on contingent facts about human psychology.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Kant thinks moral law must hold with necessity for every rational being. Ask what kind of source could guarantee that, given that observation, feeling, and tradition all vary.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> According to Kant, which of the following is the only thing that is good without qualification?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">Happiness</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Intelligence</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">A good will</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">Courage</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Kant opens the First Section by arguing that nothing can be called good without qualification except a good will. Talents and even happiness can become bad when the will using them is bad.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Kant notes that intelligence, courage, and even happiness can be put to bad uses. Ask what remains good no matter how circumstances turn out.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> A shopkeeper who refuses to cheat customers because dishonesty would hurt business is, in Kant's terms:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">Acting from duty</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">Acting in accordance with duty, but not from duty</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">Acting from a good will</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">Violating the categorical imperative</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The shopkeeper does what duty requires, but from self-interest. His action conforms to duty without being done from duty, so it has no moral worth on Kant's view.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Kant distinguishes acting in accordance with duty from acting from duty. Ask what actually motivates this shopkeeper's honesty.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> Kant discusses a case of a person who is naturally cold and indifferent to others but who still helps them out of duty. Kant's view is that this person's helpful action:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">Lacks moral worth, because genuine moral action requires warm feeling toward others</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">Has moral worth precisely because it is motivated by duty rather than by inclination</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">Is hypocritical and therefore morally problematic</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">Has the same moral worth as identical helpful action done from warm feeling</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. For Kant, moral worth lies in the motive. The cold person who helps because it is their duty shows the good will more clearly than someone who helps because they enjoy it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Kant locates moral worth in the motive, not in feeling. Ask what is moving the person who helps despite having no inclination to.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> "If you want to be healthy, you should exercise." In Kant's terminology, this is an example of:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">A categorical imperative</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">A hypothetical imperative</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">A perfect duty</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">A maxim that fails universalization</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The command applies only if you want the end, here being healthy. That makes it a hypothetical imperative.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Notice the 'if.' Ask whether this command would still bind you if you did not care about being healthy.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> According to Kant, the key difference between hypothetical and categorical imperatives is that:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">Hypothetical imperatives are about ethics, while categorical imperatives are about practical skills</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">Hypothetical imperatives are binding only if you want some specific end, while categorical imperatives are binding unconditionally</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">Hypothetical imperatives apply to individuals, while categorical imperatives apply to groups</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">Hypothetical imperatives are optional, while categorical imperatives are enforced by law</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Hypothetical imperatives depend on what you happen to want. The categorical imperative binds unconditionally, whatever your goals.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The difference is not about subject matter or legal enforcement. Ask whether the imperative depends on some end you happen to want.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> For Kant, a maxim is:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">A famous saying or proverb</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">A universal law of nature</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">The general principle on which an agent is actually acting</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">An imperative given by God</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A maxim is the subjective principle you are actually acting on, the 'when I am in situation S, I will do A in order to get E' behind an action.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">In Kant's technical sense a maxim is not a proverb or a law. It is the principle behind a particular agent's action. Revisit the categorical imperative page for examples.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> The universalization formulation of the categorical imperative holds that a maxim is morally permissible only if:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">It produces the greatest good for the greatest number</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">It aligns with the speaker's cultural or religious tradition</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">You can consistently will it as a universal law followed by all rational beings</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">It is one you would be willing to act on yourself</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The universalization test asks whether you could consistently will your maxim to become a universal law that every rational being follows.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">This formulation is a consistency test, not a calculation of consequences or an appeal to tradition. Ask what it means to will your maxim as a law for everyone.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> The Humanity formulation of the categorical imperative tells us:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Never use another person for any purpose whatsoever</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Treat humanity never merely as a means, but always also as an end</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Always put the interests of humanity above your own</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">Treat humanity as the ultimate source of moral law</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. We may use others as means, as we do whenever we buy something. What we may never do is treat a person merely as a means, ignoring their status as a rational end in themselves.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Notice the word 'merely.' Kant does not forbid all use of others. Ask what he says we must also always do.</div>
  </div>
</div>

